Sail:
  riscv_sim_rv32d --pmp-count=16 project | tee log
This runs the RISC-V binary file and logs it behavior, which we put into a file named log. 
It allowed us to observe the virtual memory setup and transitions in detail.

Questa(Wally0):
  wsim rv32gc --elf project --gui

This launches the squarewaves of the system where we could watch memory accesses, register changes, and instruction execution in real time. 
It made it easier to show that our memory mappings were correct.

Log:
The Sail simulation shows that the program starts running at address 0x80000000 and that the system allowed access to memory.
It shows that memory protection settings were configured to allow full read, write, and execute permissions, which prevents any access issues when switching from machine mode to supervisor mode. 
Once in supervisor mode, virtual memory is turned on by writing to the satp register. 
The value written confirms that the page table was located correctly and that the Sv32 virtual memory system was enabled.

Later in the simulation, the program writes the number 42 to the virtual address 0x90000300. 
The log shows that this value ends up at the physical address 0x80000300, which proves that both virtual addresses were correctly mapped to the same physical location. 
At the end of the run, the program writes to tohost, which tells the simulator that it has finished successfully. 
The log ends with a SUCCESS. 

Issues with Project:
Initially we had issues with page faulting in our code. At first we had no contents in our page table.
Once we added our page tables, the file was still faulting and our values were not in memory range.
We then changed our satp and PMP, since they weren't set up correctly.This successfuly ran our code.
